.TH CJSON_CREATE_NULL_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_create_null_mp, cjson_create_bool_mp, cjson_create_int_mp, cjson_create_double_mp, cjson_create_string_mp, cjson_create_list_mp, cjson_create_dictionary_mp, cjson_create_null, cjson_create_bool, cjson_create_int, cjson_create_double, cjson_create_string, cjson_create_list, cjson_create_dictionary \- allocate DOM nodes with a custom or default allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

cjson cjson_create_null_mp(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cjson cjson_create_bool_mp(bool val, ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cjson cjson_create_int_mp(long long val, ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cjson cjson_create_double_mp(double val, ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cjson cjson_create_string_mp(const char *val, ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cjson cjson_create_list_mp(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cjson cjson_create_dictionary_mp(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);

/* convenience wrappers; default allocator (NULL mp) */
static inline cjson cjson_create_null(void);
static inline cjson cjson_create_bool(bool val);
static inline cjson cjson_create_int(long long val);
static inline cjson cjson_create_double(double val);
static inline cjson cjson_create_string(const char *val);
static inline cjson cjson_create_list(void);
static inline cjson cjson_create_dictionary(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Each _mp function allocates a new DOM node of the matching JSON type
with mp as its allocator.  A NULL mp selects the default allocator
(malloc/free/calloc/realloc).  The node keeps that allocator and the
library uses it for every later operation on the node, destruction
included.

The functions without _mp are static inline wrappers that call the
_mp function with NULL as the allocator.

cjson_create_string_mp() makes a private heap copy of val.  A NULL
val gives a CJSON_NULL node, because a null C string pointer maps to
JSON null; you never get a CJSON_STRING node that holds a null
pointer.  A val that is not NULL must be valid UTF-8.  JSON text is
UTF-8 (RFC 8259 section 8.1) and any other byte sequence has no JSON
form, so cjson_create_string_mp() returns NULL for it instead of
storing it, the same way cjson_create_double_mp() treats a double
that is not finite.  cjson_set() applies the same rule when you
write a string field, but it returns ccol_invalid_args.
cjson_create_list_mp() returns an empty array, and you add children
to it with cjson_list_push().
cjson_create_dictionary_mp() returns an empty object, and you add
children to it with cjson_dictionary_set().  An empty array or
object is a single node.  The storage for its children is allocated
by its first child, so the first push or set is the call that can
fail for lack of memory.

cjson_create_double_mp() refuses Inf, -Inf and NaN and returns NULL
for them, because JSON has no form for a double that is not finite.
The parser likewise refuses a number that overflows to infinity.
cjson_set() makes the same check when you write a double field and
returns ccol_invalid_args when it fails.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp   Custom memory management procedures for this node and the
     resources it owns, or NULL for the default allocator.
     The library keeps its own copy of the struct, so the caller may
     release or reuse the struct once this call returns.  The
     functions that it names must stay callable until the last node
     built with them is destroyed.  A process can use at most 64
     distinct sets of four functions, and a call that would need a
     65th fails and returns NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new cjson node on success.  NULL when the allocation fails, when
val is not finite (cjson_create_double_mp), or when val is not valid
UTF-8 (cjson_create_string_mp).
cjson_create_string_mp(NULL, mp) returns a CJSON_NULL node, and it
returns NULL only when it runs out of memory.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The caller owns the returned node and must free it with
cjson_destroy().  That call needs no mp parameter, because it uses
the allocator that each node keeps.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_destroy (3),
.BR cjson_list_push (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_set (3)
